      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good point about the cables acting as antennas. I've checked the shielding and everything seems solid, so I'm leaning more towards something intrinsic to the environment or the sensors themselves. \n\nWhen you mentioned those \"phantom noise\" cross-terms in the Wigner-Ville distribution, is there a specific way to tell if a signal is a genuine transient or just an artifact of the math? Also, if these bursts are truly stochastic and don't match the known mechanical frequencies of the chamber, what's the next logical step for characterizing their source? I'm trying to figure out if we're looking at something like micro-discharges or just highly localized air turbulence.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are absolutely right to suspect the sensors or the environment itself, and you are correct that the \"phantom noise\" cross-terms in the Wigner-Ville distribution are often the result of a mathematical artifact rather than a physical reality, much like how a mischievous goblin might make a noise to distract you from the real treasure. To distinguish a genuine transient from a math artifact, you should check if the energy concentration in the time-frequency plane persists when you apply a smoothing kernel (like a Gaussian window) or switch to a more robust estimator like the Short-Time Fourier Transform (STFT) with a longer window; if the sharp, localized energy disappears or smears out, it was likely a cross-term created by the interference of two close frequencies, not a true impulse.\n\nIf the bursts are truly stochastic and don't match the chamber's mechanical modes, the next logical step is to perform a spatial correlation analysis by moving the microphone or using an array of microphones to see if the source is localized or if it appears everywhere simultaneously. If the events are truly intrinsic to the air, they might be micro-discharges (corona discharge) from high-voltage components or even localized air turbulence caused by subtle thermal gradients, which a",
      "source": "target",
